-- title:
--   Archimedean signs of a narrow ray class symbol
-- statement:
--   Let $K$ be a number field with ring of integers $\mathcal{O}_K$, let $M$ be a commutative group, let $\psi$ be an arbitrary function from the height one primes of $\mathcal{O}_K$ to $M$, and let $\mathfrak{f}$ be a non-zero ideal of $\mathcal{O}_K$. For a fractional ideal $I$ of $K$ write $\mathrm{raySymbol}(\psi, I) = \prod_{v}^{\ast} \psi(v)^{\mathrm{count}_v(I)}$, the finitary product over the height one primes $v$ of $\mathcal{O}_K$ of $\psi(v)$ raised to the valuation of $I$ at $v$. Assume that $\mathrm{raySymbol}(\psi, (\alpha)) = 1$ for every $\alpha \in \mathcal{O}_K$ with $\alpha \neq 0$, $\alpha - 1 \in \mathfrak{f}$, and $\tau(\alpha) > 0$ for every ring homomorphism $\tau : K \to \mathbb{R}$, the principal ideal $\mathrm{span}\{\alpha\}$ being regarded as a fractional ideal. The conclusion is that there exists a function $\varepsilon$ from the real embeddings of $K$ to $M$ such that $\varepsilon(\varphi)^2 = 1$ for every $\varphi$, and such that for every non-zero $\alpha \in \mathcal{O}_K$ with $\alpha - 1 \in \mathfrak{f}$ one has $\mathrm{raySymbol}(\psi, (\alpha)) = \prod_{\varphi} \varepsilon(\varphi)$, the product being over those real embeddings $\varphi$ of $K$ with $\varphi(\alpha) < 0$.
--
--   This is the passage from a character of the ray class group modulo $\mathfrak{f}$ to the narrow ray class group modulo $\mathfrak{f}\infty$: the values $\varepsilon(\varphi)$ are the archimedean signs of the character, so that the ray symbol of a principal ideal generated by $\alpha \equiv 1 \pmod{\mathfrak{f}}$ is determined by the signs of $\alpha$ at the real places. It is used in the construction of automorphic data attached to such a character in the Langlands–Tunnell input to the argument.
